Background. For an infinite cardinal κ, write c = 2^{ℵ_0} for the cardinality of the continuum. For cardinals α,β,γ,δ, the strong polarized partition relation
   ⎛ α ⎞      ⎛ γ ⎞^{1,1}
   ⎝ β ⎠  →  ⎝ δ ⎠_{2}
asserts that for every coloring c : α × β → 2 there exist A ⊆ α with |A| = γ and B ⊆ β with |B| = δ such that c is constant on A × B.

For an uncountable cardinal κ, say that κ belongs to the positive spectrum (of strong polarized relations) if
   ⎛ κ ⎞      ⎛ κ ⎞^{1,1}
   ⎝ ω ⎠  →  ⎝ ω ⎠_{2}
holds. A cardinal κ is called a breaking point of the positive spectrum if κ lies in the positive spectrum but there exists a cardinal μ < κ that does not lie in the positive spectrum (equivalently, κ marks an "upward jump" in the spectrum from a failure to a success).

Question. 
(a) Is it consistent with ZFC that ℵ_ω is the unique breaking point of the positive spectrum?
(b) More generally, is it consistent with ZFC that the continuum c = 2^{ℵ_0} is arbitrarily large while the set of breaking points of the positive spectrum coincides exactly with the class of cardinals of countable cofinality?

